A||||85||Tonker||Grammar, spelling and other mythical creatures.||||4||
Z||000000||Tonker||11-17-2001||04:39 AM||||<FONT SIZE="3">Today's Rant</FONT><P><u>The Issue</u><BR>You want to know what really irks me? Too bad, I'm going to tell you anyway. People who refuse to make any attempt to use grammar or correct spelling on the Internet. These concepts exist for a purpose: <I>to facilitate written communication</I>; such as one usually finds on the Internet. I can't count the number of times that I've been reading a posting or an article on the 'Net when all of a sudden, my train of thought comes to a screeching halt at the sight of something akin to the following:<P><u>The Horror</u><BR><B>I SAY THAT IM RIGHT BECUAS U KNOW YOU CANT DENY THE FORCE OF MY ARGUMENTS<BR>EVIL EXISTS BECUAS WE LET IT AND BECUAS THE BAD PEOPLE ARE NOT ERADICATED WHEN WE HAVE THE CHANCE THIS IS A VERY BAD THING.<BR>WHY SHOULD WE LET PEPLE SMOKE IN OUR HALLS WHEN EVERY DAY CHICKENS ARE HAVING THEIR EGGS STOLENED FROM RIGHT BENEATH THEM BY THE PEPLE THEY TRUST MOST YES FARMERS<BR>FARMERS ARE EVIL BECUAS THEY STEEL THE MILK AND THE EGGS AND THE LIVES OF THE ANIMALS</B><P><I>Werd dud3<BR>U h17 i7 0N the h34d wi7h yo 1337 werdz m4n<BR>P34c3 8r0!</I><P><u>What Can We Do?</u><BR>First of all, spelling. There are a number of excellent tools to assist with spelling. For example: <A HREF="http://www.webster.com/home.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.webster.com/home.htm</A>   Small and occasional spelling mistakes are unfortunate but understandable. Chronically and egregiously poor spelling is irritating because I have to stop and try to figure out which (not witch) particular meaning the author had initially intended then  place it in the context of their word-jumbles. This latter task would be greatly facilitated by the proper application of grammar.<P>Grammar (not to be confused with Grammer - the star of Frasier) is a wonderful tool. It allows a person to transform their word-jumbles into coherent thoughts. Periods ('.' for those who haven't yet been introduced) are generally used to separate one complete idea, thought or concept from the next. This is usually called a sentence. Writing in sentences greatly improves the absorption of concepts by the reader. Commas (',') are used for natural pauses within one's sentences. While not strictly necessary for comprehension, it does improve the overall flow and readability. There are many other constructs (*with big, pointy teeth like this*) that needn't bother you too much at this time.<P>To take things one step further, we might also look into the use of paragraphs. Paragraphs are groupings of logically related sentences. When used properly, paragraphs can improve the flow of your written communication immeasurably. Paragraphs can also be written in such a way that lead naturally into the next paragraph. This is a good thing.<P><u>Here's Why</u><BR>With these concepts in mind, let's look at why we should attempt to write clearly and concisely. In the "real" world, appearances do matter. You wouldn't have much confidence in a leader, the President for example, who appeared before you sporting baggy shorts, a stained muscle shirt and sandals, would you? Likewise if you were talking to a person and he began mumbling and drooling over himself, you would probably have trouble focusing in on his message. The Internet is no different. Since we do not deal in a face to face medium here, our impression is based on the message we see before us. If the message is poorly constructed and sloppy, it reflects poorly on the author. Furthermore and perhaps most importantly, the message may not be understood by the reader. Since the point of communication is to be understood it behooves us to write properly.<P><u>Excuses, Excuses.</u><BR>I've heard a number of excuses for why people write in a sloppy fashion:<BR>1. I was in a hurry.<BR>2. It's <I>their</I> problem if they don't know what I mean.<BR>3. I was never taught how to write properly.<BR>4. I'm too stupid.<BR>...<P><u>My response</u><BR>1. So find the time to do it properly, or fix it later when you have time.<BR>2. It's not my responsibility to figure out what you mean. It's your job as an author to convey your message to me clearly and concisely.<BR>3. You may not have been taught, but you didn't bother to learn either, did you?<BR>4. Yes, most likely, but give it a try anyway.<P><u>Springer's Final Thought</u><BR>We've been through a lot tonight. Some very difficult and taxing concepts. Putting them into use is the next step. While it may seem painful in the short term, you will reap many long term benefits. Okay, maybe you won't, but at least I won't have to stay up 'til 4:30 a.m. writing a rant about writing...<P>ps. It occurs to me that this probably, to some degree, borrows from a post I saw last week on here. Was it by Stimpy? Hmmm.<P>pps. "It's" is a contraction, meaning "It is". "Its" is possessive, meaning "belonging to 'it'". Just thought you might want to know.<P>ppps I'm not pointing any fingers at anyone here.<P>pppps P34c3 8r0z!<P>ppppps OH AND CAPS REALLY SUCKS FOR READING, UNLESS YOU'RE HIGHLIGHTING A POINT.<p>[This message has been edited by Tonker (edited 11-17-2001).]||||reg||4
