Z||000022||LZeitgeist||11-18-2001||01:23 PM||||When communicating through the written word (as we do here) an indelible picture of the writer is formed in the mind of the reader. <P>How do writers here desire the recipients of their messages to 'see' them in their mind's eyes?  Do you wish to appear as a knowing, coherent and intelligent individual, or do you prefer to be seen as an ignoramus?<P>When using the written word for communication, a well-spoken and intelligent message invariably carries more weight when read than a barely comprehensible one. <P>If you want to be heard, speak clearly. If you are unlearned, *LEARN*. Knowledge is power... clarity of speech and thought is strength. And one's intelligence (or lack thereof) reflects on no one except the bearer... ||||reg||1
Z||000023||Cooter||11-18-2001||01:33 PM||||Amen!||||reg||1
Z||000024||Mach10||11-18-2001||02:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LZeitgeist:<BR><B>When communicating through the written word (as we do here) an indelible picture of the writer is formed in the mind of the reader. <P>How do writers here desire the recipients of their messages to 'see' them in their mind's eyes?  Do you wish to appear as a knowing, coherent and intelligent individual, or do you prefer to be seen as an ignoramus?<P>When using the written word for communication, a well-spoken and intelligent message invariably carries more weight when read than a barely comprehensible one. <P>If you want to be heard, speak clearly. If you are unlearned, *LEARN*. Knowledge is power... clarity of speech and thought is strength. And one's intelligence (or lack thereof) reflects on no one except the bearer... </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> So you're a 65-year old, balding, white-haired university prof?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>It's presumptious to judge someone based entirely on their grammatical skills. I think you get a much clearer picture by looking at the CONTENT. A 5000-word essay, eloquently written, but containing nothing but racial slurs will FAIL. A 5000-word essay, that has excellent, constructive arguements, but several glaring grammatical mistakes? B+ instead of the A.<P>GRAMMAR/SPELLING IS NOT WORTH DOG'S SNOT. It's what you have to say that matters, not HOW you say it. <BR>||||reg||13
Z||000025||Cliff Pennock||11-18-2001||02:37 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>GRAMMAR/SPELLING IS NOT WORTH DOG'S SNOT. It's what you have to say that matters, not HOW you say it. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I disagree. You are trying to communicate using written words so it's imperative you try to be as comprehensive as possible. When someone <I>speaks</I> to me, I find it just as annoying if he speaks with his hand in front of his mouth, or if he is eating at the same time. It makes it much harder to understand what it is somebody is trying to say.<P>It all has to do with the way people actually read. We don't read letters, we read words. We recognize words, or complete parts of sentences. This enables us to read relatively fast. If we can't instantly recognize words anymore, we look at the individual letters and do a "close match" (this is all done subconscious btw). This slows down the reading process and consumes a lot of energy. It's actually true that you can get tired of reading large pieces of text with poorly constructed sentences or misspelled words.<P>Don't get me wrong, I'm not saying that I'm taking the contents of a message (or the person who composed the message) less serious if words are spelled wrong or is full with bad grammar. I just find it annoying because it makes it so much harder to read. It's the difference between an unsharp photo and a sharp photo (does that make sense?).<P>PS: Anybody else using the Spell Check option in this thread before posting a message while never doing so in other threads?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000026||Mach10||11-18-2001||02:55 PM||||I never use spell check... EVER.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Cliff: I know what your saying. There *is* something to be said about clarity. To varying degrees, spelling, grammar, and puctuation play a large part. However, to judge an argument based soley on perfect spelling/puctuation is not right. To use your metaphor, eating with hands in front of mouth, and eating. True. but what if he has an annoying/screechy voice? Cleft palate? Or is partially deaf? The words are there, but they aren't consistant with the "rest" of society.<P>With the internet, the only way most people will know you is through a bunch of 14pt Times New Roman letters, words, and phrases. The way you spell, puctuate (or not), or assemble your sentences is VERY much a part of your personality. My grammar isn't perfect. I have decent, but not perfect spelling. But there's no way in hell I'm going to let an algorithm destroy my own style. True, it may be easier to read than others. Well, some people just speak in tones that are easier to distinguish. In life, I tend to talk a little fast. But you wouldn't be rude enough to tell me slow down at every word, would you?<P>We're both going to have our opinions. *I* think that as long as it's still legible, then all it serves is to add character to a post...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>But then, I'm usually out in left feild picking daisies  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000027||Cliff Pennock||11-18-2001||03:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>Cliff: I know what your saying. There *is* something to be said about clarity. To varying degrees, spelling, grammar, and puctuation play a large part. However, to judge an argument based soley on perfect spelling/puctuation is not right. To use your metaphor, eating with hands in front of mouth, and eating. True. but what if he has an annoying/screechy voice? Cleft palate? Or is partially deaf? The words are there, but they aren't consistant with the "rest" of society.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's why I'm saying I <u>don't</u> take the message or person <u>less</u> serious. It's just annoying.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>And I still find it unforgiving if english speaking people <u>consistently</u> spell basic english words wrong, because that <u>does</u> tell you something about someone.<P>Could also be because one of the things I do for a living is copy-writing as well as editing text. So I'm used to stop at each misspelled word.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000028||maryjane||11-18-2001||03:18 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<P>GRAMMAR/SPELLING IS NOT WORTH DOG'S SNOT. It's what you have to say that matters, not HOW you say it. [/B]<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Well, the way we say things usually affect how they are being interpreted on the receiving end. I understand who you are defending and why. What if every post by everyone was like that? Be pretty difficult reading. This type post is self-defeating, as people figure it was not really written to be read or understood, only to be written for the writer's self-indulgence. It's hard to try to respond seriously to a post that the writter has done things in, that he knows are offensive (grammatically and spelling-wise) to the majority of the readers. People are much more willing to answer questions when they can understand them, not have to run their own spell check in their minds. Had our recently departed member used more eloquence and tact in his statements, his views probably wouldn't have been taken quite as offensively. It wasn't necessarily WHAT he said, but the blatant & offensive way in which he said them that caused problems. <BR>No, I usually don't run spell check. For some reason I have lost text when I click the "finish checking" button.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000029||maryjane||11-18-2001||03:52 PM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by Mach10:<BR>[B]I never use spell check... EVER.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>...There's no way in hell I'm going to let an algorithm destroy my own style. True, it may be easier to read than others. Well, some people just speak in tones that are easier to distinguish. In life, I tend to talk a little fast. But you wouldn't be rude enough to tell me slow down at every word, would you?<P><BR>If I knew you were doing it just to be different or to be offensive, yes; I would definitly ask you to speak slower, or in this case, to type coherently. If it is being done only in certain contexts, it is pretty apparent that it is being done for the writers benefit only. Some people do these things simply for the aggravation value. Don't think that shows much character at all. Know whut I mean, Vern? I don't think I'm in the minority here, but may be. I try to stay in right field, myself.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000030||Mach10||11-18-2001||04:01 PM||||I ain't defending ANYBODY. Unless they are blatantly speaking in 1337-$p34K, I don't say ANYTHING. I attacked Eds for the same thing, and I will do it to anyone that puts down someone's argument soley on the basis of grammar/spelling. <P>Yes, I would agree that how you say it affects the meaning of your post. What I am saying, though, is that attitude, and content are far more important that anctual wording. <P>MJ: Check your PMs  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 11-18-2001).]||||reg||7
Z||000031||TRiAD||11-18-2001||04:47 PM||||Talking while eating is rude, and can be fixed, while a cleft palate or hearing deficiency is invoulentary and cannot be helped. It has nothing to do with your Rush Limbaugh "blending in with society" crap. <P>There's a difference; it's called INTENT. ||||reg||13
Z||000032||Mach10||11-18-2001||05:29 PM||||   <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B>Talking while eating is rude, and can be fixed, while a cleft palate or hearing deficiency is invoulentary and cannot be helped. It has nothing to do with your Rush Limbaugh "blending in with society" crap. <P>There's a difference; it's called INTENT. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>so? if your typing/writing skills aren't up to "par" then you have a moral obligation to improve them? the 13 year old nihilist talking exclusively in leet-speek[sic] is one thing. someone who doesn't cross tees and dot eyes is totally different. i fail to see what's rude and/or offensive about having poor literary skills, to a point. maniak84 for example is pushing the limits but even then is not crossing them...<P>edit: i want to clarify. i don't think that having poor literary skills is a disability, i was only putting them in the same context. my point is that it's another form of variety, and as long as it's legible, how is it hurting anyone?<P>edit-edit: oh, and i will admit. all-caps are *really* annoying  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 11-18-2001).]<p>[This message has been edited by Mach10 (edited 11-18-2001).]||||reg||7
