Z||000042||ChopTop||11-19-2001||11:06 AM||||I believe learning how to use English grammar correctly is extremely difficult.  Even experts sometimes disagree on the hundreds of rules that govern our language.  Adding to the confusion is grammar reference books.  Designed to help us become better writers; they sometimes end up confusing us more.  And since there are several different reference books available, it's up to the writer to decide on which publication's interpretation to believe.  In addition, grammar rules change in time, what may not have been accepted 20 years ago may be fine today.  <BR>Maybe that's why I don't mind reading misspelled words in a posting, or no caps, or periods.  I believe it's more important to read for content, than it is for grammar skills.  But I will agree that a well written document is easier for a reader to understand.  However, I don't believe a person's grammar skills is necessarily a reflection of their intelligence level.        ||||reg||1
Z||000043||Mach10||11-19-2001||11:21 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ChopTop:<BR><B>I believe learning how to use English grammar correctly is extremely difficult.  Even experts sometimes disagree on the hundreds of rules that govern our language.  Adding to the confusion is grammar reference books.  Designed to help us become better writers; they sometimes end up confusing us more.  And since there are several different reference books available, it's up to the writer to decide on which publication's interpretation to believe.  In addition, grammar rules change in time, what may not have been accepted 20 years ago may be fine today.  <BR>Maybe that's why I don't mind reading misspelled words in a posting, or no caps, or periods.  I believe it's more important to read for content, than it is for grammar skills.  But I will agree that a well written document is easier for a reader to understand.  However, I don't believe a person's grammar skills is necessarily a reflection of their intelligence level.        </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's how I feel. <P>||||reg||14
Z||000044||LZeitgeist||11-19-2001||11:29 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>However, I don't believe a person's grammar skills is necessarily a reflection of their intelligence level. <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>When reading the written word, grammar skills are a great deal of what there is to go on. Yes, content is equally (if not more) important, but if the writer isn't willing to take the time to write the content of their message clearly, then the average reader will be willing to take even *less* time to attempt to decypher what the (evidently unconcerned) writer is making a seemingly poor attempt to convey.<P><BR>(BTW - it's "However, I don't believe a person's grammar skills <B>ARE</B> necessarily a reflection of their intelligence level."  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> (j/k)) ||||reg||1
Z||000045||LZeitgeist||11-19-2001||11:36 AM||||Forgot to add my summary explanation (please insert between above paragraph and joke):<P>In other words, if you care so little about what you are writing that you can't take the time to make it coherent, why should I care enough to have to take the time to try and make it out? After all, you, as the writer, are attempting to convey a message to me, as the reader - why should <B>I</B> be required to put in more effort than <B>you</B> in making <B>your</B> message heard? (This mindset, of course, varies greatly due to content... if I'm in a desert and you are giving me misspelled directions on where to find a life-saving glass of water, then the content becomes more important than the grammar...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">)||||reg||1
Z||000046||Tonker||11-19-2001||11:50 AM||||Wow, this thread is up to 45 replies already?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>My main gripe is not with the lack of proper grammar and spelling, it's with the lack of effort. A little bit of effort can go a long way to making text more readable. I don't expect to see perfectly written posts and messages all the time. I certainly make my fair share of mistakes. I also understand that some people have disabilities or speak english as a second(or third or...) language. I <B>do</B> expect that if someone is going to write something, that they take the the 10 seconds to ensure that they have at least placed a period at the end of their sentences. It also takes little effort or time to run a spell checker if you know that your strength is not in spelling.<BR>||||reg||7
Z||000047||LZeitgeist||11-19-2001||12:26 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>My main gripe is not with the lack of proper grammar and spelling, it's with the lack of effort. A little bit of effort can go a long way to making text more readable. <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR><B>YES!!!!</B> Exactly!||||reg||1
Z||000048||btoth||11-19-2001||12:57 PM||||My biggest complaints:  (Most of these apply to internet forums/email, and not hand-written letters, business papers, etc.)<P>1.)  All caps.  It's OK if your emphasizing a word or two (using <B>bold</B> or <I>itallics</I> is better), or if you really are intending to yell at someone.  Otherwise, proper capitalization helps, or even all lowercase is easier to read.<P>2.)  Punctuation.  I don't care how well you know grammer (I slept throught my English classes) but most people should know where a period goes.  Comma's are a plus, but aren't <I>as</I> important as periods.<P>3.)  Please break up your long blocks of text with a blank line to easily distinguish different thoughts/topics in the same post.  This is optional depending on how long your <B>one</B> paragraph really is, I guess.<P>4.)  I've noticed that some people seem to hit their ENTER key after every sentance and sometimes in the middle of sentances... what's up with that?  It's called <B>word-wrap</B> (at least in computer terms).  Just keep typing 'till the end of a paragraph.  (Unless your making a list, obviously.)<P>OK, I think I'm throught b****ing now.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by btoth (edited 11-19-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000049||Butter||11-19-2001||01:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Tonker:<BR>I can't count the number of times that I've been reading a posting or an article on the 'Net when all of a sudden, my train of thought comes to a screeching halt at the sight of something akin to the following:<P><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Must be a sh0rt train.  You c, I r Hillbilly & i no u can't stop a train reel fast.  My 1st grade teacher got runned over by a train (God bless her soul).  I reckon' her train of thankin' wus gettin' sh0rt cus it stopped everthang she wus duin'.  That wus the las thang she learned me, "Don't run in front of a movin' train".  She wus a sweet old woman.  I red about her train reck in the paper and reckoned bout how many folks would read this that she learned how 2 read.  O hear cums my train again.  Now if you need help with the Hillbilly translations I R Hear 4 u.  Your Welcum.<BR>||||reg||12
