Z||000072||JSocha||11-20-2001||03:41 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Voytek:<BR><B>Yes, there is the other side of the coin.  The 'big' words (we've seen a member use an abundance of those).  It is not a sign of intelligence - it's a sign of a pea-brain trying to look bigger and superior.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I resent that comment...unless of course, you are talking about a "former" member.  However, if you are talking about mwa...I just did not want 84Bill's new, unused books going to waste.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> hehehehe  I said I would!<P>However, just because a person can not spell or type correctly, regardless of reason, does not mean that they are any less intelligent then you, the person sitting next to you or me.<P>By the way, since you as well are from Canada.  Is it "color" or "colour"? Or, how do you pronounce or enunciate the word "schedule"?<P>Would it make you or the other person spelling or saying those words any less intelligent in the way they do, even if both are correct?||||reg||7
Z||000073||Cliff Pennock||11-20-2001||03:47 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Return of Fiero:<BR><B>along the lines, someone said "wasted money on education." was that cliff?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yep, that was me. But to be exact, I said: "but if you <u>consistently</u> spell the most <u>basic</u> words wrong, well, then you're either dyslexic, or your education was a total waste of money." (added underlining)<P>I don't believe for one second you can be a rocket-scientist while consistently spelling the most simplest words wrong. I'm not talking about getting the spelling wrong <B>once</B> - because that happens, I'm talking about spelling the same simple words wrong all through a lenghty message.<P>M(y/i)ke, I stopped reading your post halfway through because the spelling errors were annoying me as hell. Does that mean I don't take you seriously? No, it just means I chose not to read your message any further. Nothing more, nothing less. If you want to get your message across to as many people as possible, then it's <B>your</B> responsibility to make it as comprehensible as possible. It takes little effort to correct spelling and I can only guess to the reasons why people choose to not even try.<BR><p>[This message has been edited by Cliff Pennock (edited 11-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000074||JSocha||11-20-2001||04:24 PM||||Albert Einstein was a genius in his time, yet he coudn't even remember his own phone number, could only communicate mathmatically and had a severe problem spelling...more then likely because of his ethnic background.<P>For example, my father who came from Germany originally, did not speak a word of English when he arrived in Canada until years later...self taught. Was successful in business thereafter for over 40 years, 30 of which he owned and operated his own business.  Yet he couldn't spell to save his life and yet could communicate effectively with what he had to say.<P>Yet he spelled common words such as the word "much"--&gt;"mutch".  The word "the"--&gt;"thee" or "thah"; "funny"--&gt;"funnee" or "funnie"; "of"--&gt;"ouv", etc...<P>Of course I don't here his accent like most people do as I am tone deaf to it. However, when he talks as his method of communication, the way he pronounces words because of his German ethnic background doesn't make him any less intelligent then the person he is talking to with a college education or Phd in the English language.<P>Yet, by the standards of acceptance presented here in this thread, anyone here seeminly can be labeled an "imbecile" if they do not fall within the tolerant level of others for that individuals shortcomings.<P>We are all not perfect.  We all make mistakes of some sort, be it spelling, grammer, the way we talk, the way we walk, the way we work.  Some people can't even walk a straight line.  And if we think we are perfect...well...that's another thread of discussion.<P>The Japanese label people with handicaps as inferior if they are born "imperfect" and we in our society look at that as a form of demeaning segregation.<P>Here's another example, which floored me not to mention my wife.  Three years ago for our honeymoon, we took a trip aboard Holland America through the Carribean.  Of course they are staffed with people from other countries.<P>On one of the shuttle buses from the shore back to the ship, one of the foreign staff members was talking to one of the passengers.  He came aboard the ship only three months prior to that cruise to work, didn't speak a word of English at the time and at the time he was talking to the passenger on the shuttle back to the ship indicated that he could (well, he had to) speak several languages fairly fluelently, but could not write the languages.  He met us once when we had boarded the ship to which my wife or I could not recall when, yet he remembered both my wife's name and mine...including our lastname and pronounced it correctly.<P>He admitted he could not write the languages he had to speak with other guests, yet he could talk it effectively.  I spoke what little German I could remember and he was correcting me on the context and he was correct.  Based on this thread, he would be a walking idiot if we applied these leavels of measures to others abilities.||||reg||9
Z||000075||LZeitgeist||11-20-2001||04:38 PM||||There is a mile-wide stretch between considering someone 'stupid' because they can't spell, and feeling that someone's message is less valuable to spend my time decyphering if they couldn't be bothered to take the time to write it coherently.<P>I see very few posts here I would attribute to stupid people. However, I read a *lot* of messages every day from evidently lazy ones. ||||reg||1
Z||000076||JSocha||11-20-2001||04:47 PM||||<FONT SIZE="3"><B><u>Writing Techniques</u></B></FONT><P><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Avoid alliteration. Always.<BR><LI>Prepositions are not words to end sentences with. <BR><LI>Avoid clichés like the plague. (They're old hat.) <BR><LI>Employ the vernacular. <BR><LI>Eschew ampersands & abbreviations, etc. <BR><LI>Parenthetical remarks (however relevant) are unnecessary. <BR><LI>It is wrong to ever split an infinitive. <BR><LI>Contractions aren't necessary. <BR><LI>Foreign words and phrases are not apropos. <BR><LI>One should never generalize. <BR><LI>Eliminate quotations. As Ralph Waldo Emerson said, "I hate quotations. Tell me what you know." <BR><LI>Comparisons are as bad as clichés. <BR><LI>Don't be redundant; don't use more words than necessary; it's highly superfluous. <BR><LI>Be more or less specific. <BR><LI>Understatement is always best. <BR><LI>One-word sentences? Eliminate. <BR><LI>Analogies in writing are like feathers on a snake. <BR><LI>The passive voice is to be avoided. <BR><LI>Go around the barn at high noon to avoid colloquialisms. <BR><LI>Even if a mixed metaphor sings, it should be derailed. <BR><LI>Who needs rhetorical questions? <BR><LI>Exaggeration is a billion times worse than understatement.<BR></UL><p>[This message has been edited by JSocha (edited 11-20-2001).]||||reg||10
Z||000077||Cliff Pennock||11-20-2001||05:12 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by JSocha:<BR><B>Based on this thread, he would be a walking idiot if we applied these leavels of measures to others abilities.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I don't read that anywhere (unless it's in those messages I have chosen not to read). If you've had at least 6 years of education in the English language and have lived all your life in an english speaking and writing society, but still spell basic words <u>consistently</u> wrong (I keep using the word "consistently" to differentiate between an occasional slip of the fingers, brain, or whatever and simply not knowing how to spell the word), then your education has been a waste of money <u>in my opinion</u>.<P>And then there are the people who couldn't care less. There are a lot of words I don't know how to spell. If there's a danger of it being misunderstood, I use the spell-checker. It takes less than a minute of <B>my</B> time but will make my message so much more understandable for others. I'm not saying people should use the spell-checker all the time, but if you <B>know</B> your message is probably filled with errors that make it incomprehensible, and there's a tool readily available to help you in this respect, then you should use it. If you balance your checkbook, do you not use your pocketcalculator?<P>Good points have been made for both sides of the story. The message itself is more important than how it is brought. <B>True</B>. But does that mean you should pay no attention at all to the message's delivery? Some geniuses couldn't spell worth sh!t. <B>True</B>. But <u>never</u> was this to be attributed to laziness or disinterest in getting it right.<P>Presentation is <B>very</B> important. How popular would this forum be if all messages were shown on a single page, with <B>no</B> physical clues as to where an old message ended or a new message began? I mean, the contents would still be the same, right? How popular would this forum be if I spelled "Fiero" as "Feiro" <B>everywhere</B>? Would you take this site seriously? Would you have even found it?<P>These are my <B>personal</B> opinions, and <B>never</B> will I enforce them upon others. I've never made a single comment about someone's spelling (here on the forum, at my office it's a different story  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) and will never do so. But I will not play devil's advocate and say I don't find it important in some respects.||||reg||1
Z||000078||JSocha||11-20-2001||05:54 PM||||At my own serious risk of potentially getting my own butt kicked off and black listed from the forum for even thinking, let alone suggesting this  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">, would it be possible to "reinstate" EdsB52?<P>I mean, he seemingly had quite the perspective to which he was willing to communicate to all and expound upon on the subject of proper grammer, spelling and the grading of people's posts much like a University Professor would if you sumitted a paper and make minor recommendations how to improve oneself's gramatical skills.<P>I mean, there should be quite a few people here that should have no problems with their grammer to be rated by someone else.<P>Or...we could all just chalk this one up as another one of a series of absolutely worthless brain farts that I seemingly have from time to time.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Please don't take me seriously on this...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||10
